# Director of Public Safety — Independent Brief

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals (IAD and DCA)
**Chair:** Director of Public Safety (sworn police, ARFF/fire, and 911/dispatch under one command)
**Date:** 2026-08-07
**Author independence:** Written without reading any other Stage 1 brief.

---

## 1. Key findings

1. **A quiet-by-design standard, correctly written, is a public safety upgrade — not a threat to it.** Lowering the ambient noise floor is the fastest engineering path to improving the Speech Transmission Index (STI) of the emergency voice/alarm communications system that NFPA 72 already requires. The failure mode is not quietness; it is a policy written as a branding gesture that reduces announcements without specifying the acoustic and intelligibility performance that must remain. If MWAA writes STI, reverberation, and signal-to-noise ratio into the design manual as measurable pass/fail criteria, the standard is defensible in an after-action review. If it writes "calm and inviting soundscape," it will not survive one.

2. **The killing risk in a modern terminal is not decibels; it is crowd behavior under ambiguous information.** Fort Lauderdale (January 2017) and JFK (August 2016) are the two operative case studies. Both events show that once passengers self-evacuate through jet-bridge exits, tarmac gates, and emergency doors, the fatality mechanism becomes trampling, hypothermia on ramps, misidentified officers, and secondary panic waves — not the original threat. Both events involved poor, contradictory, or absent authoritative communication from the operator's public-safety command. The design standard should be judged on how it performs in the eighth minute of a false active-shooter report, not on how pleasant it sounds on a Tuesday afternoon.

3. **Part 139 § 139.325 mandates a "communications network" in the Airport Emergency Plan; it does not tell MWAA how loud or how intelligible.** The regulation is silent on sound-pressure levels, STI thresholds, and paging cadence. That silence is not permission — it is the reason the local operator standard has to carry the load. If MWAA does not specify EVACS performance for new terminal space, the specification defaults to whichever design-build team writes the loudest requirement into a bid response, and that is almost never the public-safety office.

4. **The PSAP question is the single most-underappreciated public safety variable in a "quiet" terminal.** MWAA's own police department operates across three counties and two states, but wireless 911 calls from inside a terminal jet bridge do not automatically route to MWAA dispatch. Under the FCC's Location-Based Routing rule, they route to whichever PSAP the caller's device geolocation resolves to. If MWAA relies on Loudoun County or Arlington County to answer a 911 from a passenger crouched in the E-Concourse holdroom, the two-way information flow that quiet-by-design assumes — passenger to dispatch to PA/EVACS to responding officers to gate agent — is broken by an area-code accident. This deserves an explicit MWAA policy determination before the standard is drafted, not after.

5. **Redundant communication channels are already legally required; the standard should specify them, not "consider" them.** ADA/ABA and DOT's 49 CFR Part 27 require visual counterparts to audible information. A quiet-by-design standard that reduces spoken announcements without hardening visual paging on FIDS, staffed information posts, assistive-listening loops, and multilingual digital signage will produce an ADA-effective-communication violation and a public-safety failure in the same event. The good news is that these systems are cheap relative to a new terminal and their absence is indefensible.

6. **The tenant and airline enforcement gap will eat the standard alive if the U&L and tenant agreements do not carry it forward.** MWAA's 233 sworn officers do not control what a United gate agent, a TSA TDC, a CBP officer at the FIS hall, or a Hudson News clerk says or plays over their local PA. Most of the "airport announcement" burden a passenger perceives comes from these tenant systems, not the terminal PA. A design standard that stops at the concrete-and-steel envelope will look strong in a design review and mean nothing on the concourse. The standard needs contract hooks in the 2025 U&L, in tenant lease amendments, and in the airport's Rules and Regulations enforced by MWAA Police.

7. **DCA operates inside a heightened-safety political environment after the January 29, 2025 midair collision.** Any policy that appears to reduce public safety communication capacity at DCA will be read against that backdrop by the NTSB, by Congress, by the airline safety community, and by families of the 67 dead. The standard should be able to answer, in one sentence, why it makes the airport safer — not merely quieter.

8. **A quiet-by-design standard is a good place to insert the emergency-communication capital the CIP has been quietly deferring.** ARFF apparatus, portable and mobile radio replacement, and CAD/dispatch upgrades usually lose annual budget fights to airfield and terminal projects. Wrapping the EVACS, FIDS-integrated visual paging, mass-notification, and NG911 pieces into the Concourse E and expanded Dulles program budget lines is cheaper per dollar of public-safety capability than any standalone request the department will ever put forward.

