## 2. Evidence

### 2.1 The regulatory floor MWAA cannot design below

Part 139.325 requires each certificated airport to maintain an Airport Emergency Plan that includes a "communications network" and procedures to notify responders "of the location of an aircraft accident, the number of persons involved in that accident, or any other information necessary to carry out their responsibilities" [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-G/part-139/subpart-D/section-139.325]. The regulation is deliberately technology-neutral. It does not name a sound level, an STI threshold, an EVACS zoning scheme, or a paging cadence. That is why the operator design standard has to specify what "communications network" means inside a passenger terminal, or it will be specified by contractors in a bid response.

Part 139.319 requires ARFF vehicles to reach the midpoint of the most distant runway from their assigned station within three minutes of alarm under optimum visibility and weather conditions and to begin applying agent within four minutes [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/14/139.319]. That drives ARFF station geometry, apparatus replacement timing, and staffing minimums — all of which are independent of terminal acoustics, but all of which depend on the dispatch and communications chain from the caller (passenger, tower, ramp, or terminal) through the 911/CAD stack to the responding company officer. Quiet-by-design cannot break any link in that chain.

NFPA 72 (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code, 2022 edition), Chapter 24, is the technical authority for emergency voice/alarm communication systems in U.S. terminals. The intelligibility criterion is that at least 90 percent of measurement locations in each acoustically distinguishable space have an STI of not less than 0.45 and an average STI of not less than 0.50 [Source: https://www.ecmag.com/magazine/articles/article-detail/integrated-systems-use-your-inside-voice-intelligibility-requirements-fire-alarm-systems] [Source: https://www.sdmmag.com/articles/90530-measuring-voice-intelligibility-for-emergency-communications-systems]. Two operational facts follow. First, STI is a signal-to-noise measure: lowering the ambient noise floor improves STI faster and cheaper than adding loudspeakers. Second, NFPA 72 permits a "listen test" in lieu of quantitative STI measurement — which is why so many terminals have EVACS on paper and unmeasured intelligibility in practice. A serious MWAA standard should mandate quantitative STI measurement at commissioning and re-commissioning after tenant fit-out, not accept a listen test.

ADA/ABA Accessibility Standards and DOT's 49 CFR Part 27 require effective communication in airport terminals, including visual counterparts to audible information and assistive-listening capability in assembly areas. Peer airports have interpreted this as visual paging integrated with FIDS, weather-display co-location, and staffed information positions — MSP is the reference implementation [Source: https://www.mspairport.com/airport/accessibility/visual-paging-emergency-messages]. Any MWAA standard that reduces spoken announcements without hardening these visual and assistive channels will not survive an ADA complaint or an accessibility-advocate review.

### 2.2 What the false-alarm case studies actually show

The Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International Airport (FLL) active shooter incident of January 6, 2017 killed five and wounded six in Terminal 2 baggage claim. Approximately 90 minutes after the initial gunfire — after the shooter was in custody — speculation of additional shots in other areas of the airport caused a chaotic self-evacuation of thousands of passengers onto the airfield [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]. The After-Action Report produced for the Broward County Aviation Department documents that the operator's tabletop exercise program had not considered the secondary-panic scenario the airport actually faced. The report also documents specific radio-channel and dispatch coordination adjustments — statewide channel bridging, added dispatchers per channel — that were reactive rather than pre-planned. For the MWAA standard, the FLL lesson is that the public address and EVACS system is not the only communication node that matters; the credibility of authoritative public communication in the second and third hour of an incident is what stops or fuels the self-evacuation cascade.

The JFK false active shooter event of August 14, 2016 is the clearer public-safety case study on ambiguous communication. A cheering crowd watching Usain Bolt's 100-meter Olympic victory in a Terminal 8 cafe was misinterpreted as gunfire, triggering 109 emergency 911 calls, a 275-officer response, and passenger self-evacuation through emergency exits and jet bridges onto the tarmac [Source: https://abc7ny.com/post/new-report-details-how-overreaction-created-panic-in-august-scare-at-jfk-/1618407/] [Source: https://news.yahoo.com/security-agents-fueled-panic-york-airport-august-authorities-214538751.html]. The investigation attributed a significant share of the panic to untrained security personnel drawing weapons and taking "hard stands" that visibly signaled danger to passengers who had no independent source of authoritative information. The evacuation-slide "popping" sound from a Korean Air aircraft, deployed by ground personnel responding to the self-evacuation, was then reported as additional gunfire, extending the panic wave [Source: https://abc7ny.com/post/new-report-details-how-overreaction-created-panic-in-august-scare-at-jfk-/1618407/]. The Port Authority subsequently committed to emergency training for all airport workers including vendors — the same tenant-enforcement problem MWAA will face.

Both events reinforce the same design implication: quiet-by-design must be paired with an operational communications doctrine that assumes passengers will look for information in the absence of authoritative broadcast. A design standard that removes announcement redundancy without specifying who, on what channel, in what language, within how many seconds, will re-establish authoritative narrative during a crisis is not a public safety standard.

### 2.3 The PSAP and NG911 reality

FCC's Location-Based Routing for Wireless 911 Calls rule, adopted in 2024, requires wireless carriers to route 911 calls based on the caller's precise device location rather than cell-tower proximity [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/03/13/2024-03157/location-based-routing-for-wireless-911-calls]. In an NG911 architecture, that call reaches the correct PSAP through an Emergency Services IP Network, with dynamic location routing that identifies the building, floor, and room where technically supported [Source: https://nga911.com/blogs/post/beyond-911-phone-call-how-ng911-turns-every-emergency-interaction-smarter-faster-response]. This is the enabling technology for a passenger calling 911 from a jet bridge to reach the airport's own dispatch — but only if MWAA has designated its dispatch as a primary PSAP for the geofenced footprint of the terminal and enrolled the airport addresses correctly in the Emergency Services IP Network.

The MWAA Police Department is a full sworn agency of 233 officers, 40 traffic control officers, and 15 professional staff, with officers certified in the Commonwealth of Virginia and authority across multiple counties along Airport property, the Dulles Toll Road, and the Dulles Access Highway [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/policefireems/mwaa-police-department/Aboutus]. The MWAA Fire and EMS Department was formed from FAA's Aircraft Rescue and Firefighting Branch at the 1987 authority transfer [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/policefireems/mwaa-fire-ems-department]. The relevant question the quiet-by-design standard forces the operator to answer is not whether these departments exist — they do — but whether the 911 call that a frightened passenger makes from a Concourse E holdroom in October 2026 reaches an MWAA dispatcher who owns the incident, or a Loudoun County dispatcher who has to hand it off. The design standard should require that answer to be documented, tested annually, and revised as terminal footprints change.

### 2.4 The Schiphol lesson is smaller than the branding suggests

Amsterdam Schiphol's "silent airport" reputation is often cited as proof that operational risk can be reduced by policy alone. The evidence is thinner than the branding. Schiphol operates a formally silent operation for two minutes each May 4, the Dutch Remembrance Day, with air traffic control suspending taxi and landing clearances between 20:00 and 20:03, radio communication halted, and baggage systems stopped [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]. This is an intentional, choreographed ritual that could not be executed at a U.S. Part 139 airport without an equivalent FAA and TSA suspension. Day-to-day, Schiphol's "quiet" claim is grounded in reduced-announcement policy and reliance on visual and mobile channels — legitimate practice, but performed inside a single-operator European regulatory regime that MWAA does not share. MWAA operates under FAA Part 139, TSA Part 1542, CBP FIS-hall rules, and the airline signatory framework of a 2025 U&L. Regulatory transfer of announcement policy from Schiphol is not automatic and should not be assumed.

