## 3. The public safety case — for and against the thesis

### The case for a quiet-by-design standard, from the chair of public safety

A well-written standard is the best available lever to fix intelligibility problems in existing terminal space and to prevent them in new terminal space. NFPA 72 gives a floor; MWAA has been living at that floor. Concourse E opens in autumn 2026 and is the first substantial new terminal at IAD in decades. If the standard is drafted and adopted before the commissioning walk-through, the airport gets a measured STI baseline in every acoustically distinguishable space, a documented redundant-channel plan, an EVACS zoning scheme that supports credible authoritative narrative in a crisis, and a design manual clause the next project inherits automatically. If not, the airport gets whatever the design-build team specified, and the public safety office finds out after the ribbon-cutting.

The standard is also the vehicle for locking in the underfunded pieces of the public safety capital program that never win on their own. Visual paging integration on FIDS, NG911 enrollment, mass-notification, mobile push, and staffed-post signage are cheap components that lose annual capital fights against apron and gate work. Wrapping them into the terminal design standard turns a project cost line into a compliance requirement.

Finally — and this is the argument most airport executives underweight — clear operator communication in the first three minutes of an ambiguous event is the single highest-leverage public safety investment the airport can make. FLL and JFK are the proof. Neither event was made worse by a lack of sound-pressure level. Both were made worse by the absence of an authoritative operator voice that passengers trusted enough to stop running. Quiet-by-design, done right, is how you build the acoustic and channel environment where that voice is heard.

### The case against, from the same chair

A "quiet airport" written as a customer-experience initiative is a public safety liability. If the standard reads as "fewer announcements" without STI, reverberation, and channel-redundancy specifications, the department will spend the next decade defending itself against evacuation cases, ADA complaints, and after-action findings that a 27-page design guide could have prevented. The department cannot rewrite the standard from within an incident.

The standard also risks displacing the enforcement burden onto MWAA Police without giving them the tools to carry it. Tenant announcements — airline, TSA, CBP, concessions — are the largest share of passenger-perceived audio and are not controlled by the terminal PA. If the U&L and tenant leases do not carry the standard forward with contractual teeth, MWAA Police will be asked to enforce a design guide, which they cannot. Better to write the standard so that its enforcement mechanism sits in contract and design review, not in a badge.

Finally, DCA cannot be treated as a smaller IAD. Terminal 2 is a Cesar Pelli vaulted-hall building with intrinsically difficult reverberation. Terminal 1 is a 1941 Charles Goodman building on the National Register. Any standard that specifies a single set of acoustic and EVACS numbers across both airports will either under-perform at DCA or become unbuildable at IAD. The standard must be tiered — mandatory across MWAA where regulation and NFPA 72 dictate, context-dependent where terminal geometry and historic fabric require deviation with documented equivalency.

