## 4. Operational facts a strategist can use

1. **Part 139.319 three-minute ARFF response to the midpoint of the most distant runway** is the operational metric that survives every terminal debate. Any quiet-by-design decision that touches the dispatch and CAD pipeline has to be regression-tested against this response-time obligation, not just against decibel targets [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/14/139.319].

2. **NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 average and ≥ 0.45 minimum in 90 percent of measurement points, with quantitative measurement permitted but not required** is the intelligibility standard the design manual should raise to "quantitatively measured at commissioning" for MWAA — because a listen test can pass a room that will fail in a crowded terminal at Level of Service E [Source: https://www.ecmag.com/magazine/articles/article-detail/integrated-systems-use-your-inside-voice-intelligibility-requirements-fire-alarm-systems].

3. **Fort Lauderdale, January 6, 2017** — five dead in the initial event, and a chaotic secondary self-evacuation ninety minutes later on the strength of a false report the operator did not credibly correct in real time. The Broward AAR is the single best public document for how a modern hub actually behaves in the second and third hour of an active-shooter response, and it should be required reading before the standard is drafted [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf].

4. **JFK, August 14, 2016** — no shooter, no gun, 109 911 calls, 275 responding officers, and mass self-evacuation caused by an Olympic cheer, drawn weapons, and evacuation-slide pops mistaken for gunfire. The investigation's core finding was that untrained personnel and the absence of authoritative narrative caused the panic. The MWAA standard has to specify who owns that narrative and on what channels [Source: https://abc7ny.com/post/new-report-details-how-overreaction-created-panic-in-august-scare-at-jfk-/1618407/].

5. **MWAA Police Department, 233 sworn officers with authority across Airport property in three counties and two states; MWAA Fire and EMS, formed from the FAA ARFF Branch at authority transfer in 1987** — the enforcement of the standard runs through both departments, and both are under the Director of Public Safety's chain of command. The standard's contract hooks should be drafted with the Police Chief and the Fire Chief in the room, not around them [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/policefireems/mwaa-police-department/Aboutus] [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/policefireems/mwaa-fire-ems-department].

